mike winkelmann also known as beeple has  been creating a new piece of art  every day for over 13 years now he's  been making art since before  netflix had a streaming service before  toy story had a third installment  and before bitcoin even existed with his  massive  69 million dollar sale at christie's  he's now the third  most expensive living artist in the  world  people's life is an incredible story of  grit and determination  so stick with me while i take you  through it i'm john coogan  and this is the saga of mike winkelmann  aka people  people grew up in a small wisconsin town  that's home to just over 5 000 people  he didn't initially plan to be an artist  though so he went to purdue university  and studied computer science after  graduating he spent some time designing  websites for small companies  but would immerse himself in creative  projects during his off hours  in 2007 he started every days with the  goal of creating a new piece of art  every single day  on may 1st 2007 he published his first  work  a simple drawing of a man with a  mustache titled uber jay  repetition is a great way to build a new  skill slowly over time  instead of trying to binge learn  everything there is to know about  something new  and potentially burning out doing a  little bit every day  can add up slowly and help build mastery  while beeple is certainly responsible  for popularizing the idea of everydays  he actually got the idea from another  artist named tom judd  who had done a new drawing every day for  a full year  in the early years of the project beeple  bounced around between mediums  he started with just a pen and paper  moved on to cinema 4d and 3d rendering  for a while  and then spent a year on photography  before moving back into the 3d world  learning the ins and outs of a physical  camera is incredibly valuable when  you're working in 3d programs like  cinema 4d  this is because each 3d scene is  rendered using a simulated camera  and all the real world settings like  focal length aperture and shutter speed  translate essentially one to one into  the 3d world  but beeple is really known for his  cinema 4d work where he pioneered a  cyberpunky sci-fi  look that has influenced tons of other  designers who use the software  in the late 2010s people became so  popular in the cinema 4d community that  there was significant pushback against  the everyday trend that had grown out of  his initial project  essentially up and coming motion  designers felt like they had to post new  artwork  every single day in order to be taken  seriously and get work  even though the everyday process was  incredibly rewarding for people  it simply wouldn't work for everyone  just listen to how he describes his  process to nick campbell from grayscale  gorilla  something i learned is that you actually  do these every day  now i i probably like a lot of you are  like well he probably batches a few of  them  does like four on a monday and then  posts them through the week  but you actually do it every day what  why is that an important part of the  process  well it's an every day so it kind of  i would cheat so fast yeah but that's  the thing  i mean that the whole point of it is is  to get better incrementally and not try  and like i'm gonna [ __ ] cram all  night and stay up and like  it's it's about the like just kind of  spacing out the work and  being able to see that progress that  kind of obsessive work ethic clearly  paid off here  but it's certainly not the only way to  create something amazing  after all margaret mitchell spent 10  years writing dawn with the wind  which won a pulitzer prize and was  adapted into an academy award-winning  film  similarly jrl tolkien spent over a  decade writing the lord of the rings  mostly due to the fact that he still had  a full-time academic position  while he was writing the books so great  things can clearly be accomplished  regardless of whether you choose to  publish  incrementally like people or spend a  decade honing a masterpiece before  releasing it like tolkien  but publishing iteratively does have one  distinct advantage  publicity people's everyday project  gained steam very slowly at first  but it started paying off long before  any of the work was sold at auction  in 2010 he was able to collaborate with  flying lotus and  in the spirit of community collaboration  people open sourced the cinema 4d files  and allowed other 3d animators to learn  from his work  and use the 3d models in new works  people's flying lotus project was very  well received  and led to more client work with artists  like ariana grande  justin bieber childish gambino and nicki  minaj  as beeples following grew and his visual  style became more distinct  he eventually got offers to work on even  bigger projects  in 2018 louis vuitton reached out to use  some of his everydays  in their next ready-to-wear collection  the images appeared on 13 of 45 pieces  in the collection  and helped people reach a new echelon of  legitimacy but within the design  community  a controversy was stirring about whether  or not beeple had been compensated  fairly for his work  chris doe the ceo and founder of the  future  interviewed beeple and dug into the  louis vuitton deal let's take it back to  louis vuitton  okay you were a little bit drunk oh yeah  there was some alcohol involved how dare  you  it was your party so i understand you  you were talking to me it's like hey man  did you hear about the lv project i'm  like what lv project you're like man  they bought these images for me  and what do you think i would what do  you think it's a fair price to charge  and then  you showed me how much work he had done  for this thing i'm like oh it should be  this amount  and you're like oh my god no it was this  amount that's where i disagree with you  of course they have more money that they  could have paid me but  in my view and that's where i think  sometimes designers have a tendency to  overestimate their importance to a  project  in this case with louis vuitton they  didn't need me they  absolutely did not [ __ ] need me they  might have wanted me  that's very different there's a  difference for how much you'll pay for  something you want and something unique  this exchange is fascinating to me  because it reveals that  even though beeple has been honing his  craft for over a decade  he still has imposter syndrome and  doesn't understand the true value of his  art  and that's why nft auctions are such an  interesting solution to this problem  nft stands for non-fungible token  essentially a digital signature backed  by blockchain technology  that proves ownership of something  unlike bitcoin  which are all identical by design nfts  are unique  to some degree what nfts offer for sale  is the idea of scarcity  and that's perfect for people since he  releases all of his artwork for free on  instagram  and any image can be downloaded and  copied endlessly  there hasn't been a good way for him to  treat his work as fine art and sell it  as other artists would  the last six months have been incredible  for people in november of 2020  he sold an nft of his video crossroads  for  six hundred and sixty six dollars and  his reaction was pretty priceless  i was like oh my god i sold a piece  for sixty six thousand it was just like  insane in december of twenty twenty  people sold three point five million  dollars worth of art in one day  which was quickly followed by crossroads  reselling on the nft secondary market  for 6.6 million dollars of which people  got 10  then in march christie's a centuries-old  auction house  sold a 5 000 image montage by people for  nearly 70 million dollars  that makes people the third most  expensive living artist in the world  but not everyone in the art community is  happy about people's rise and newfound  legitimacy  the new york magazine art critic jerry  saltz called beeple's work  just really really derivative sci-fi and  conan and star wars crapola as far as  imagery and imagination go  whatever the medium they're just bad  kitsch even though i love beeple  i can see where salt is coming from here  beeple focuses on popular topics and has  never identified as the type of  high-minded artist  that might be embraced by the mainstream  art world and no one has been more  disparaging of people's work  than people himself just listen to how  he describes himself in this interview  well it says on your website that you  suck ass at what you do  i don't know when you wrote that but  you've been doing work many years ago  they told but it was many years ago yeah  do you still think  you suck ass i think there's a lot of  areas that i need to  work on um there's just a lot of things  that i  really know nothing about and just  squeak  my way through just hacking crap  together  but at the end of the day what's  incredible about beeple is his  dedication  he's been making a new piece of art  every day for 13 years straight  and ultimately that's what i think these  art collectors are buying  there's a reason why the collage of his  first 5 000 pieces  sold for so much more than any one  artwork  it represents the progress and focus  that beeple has brought to his work for  years and years  in some ways every day's the first five  thousand days  is more performance art than digital art  anyone could compile five thousand  images into a collage  but only people could create this  specific collage and what it represents  people's work is the ultimate refutation  of the my kid could make that argument  that people often levy when art sells  for exorbitant prices  sure your kid probably could learn  cinema 4d and start pumping out renders  but by the time they had 5000 every day  is published they  probably wouldn't even be a kid anymore  and that's gotta be worth something
 

